{"name":"Blue Moondust","hex":"#376a93","fetched":"2026-10-24","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"moondust","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fine regolith found on the surface of the Moon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moondust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lunar regolith","blocks":[{"text":"Lunar regolith is the unconsolidated material found on the surface of the Moon and in the Moon's tenuous atmosphere. Lunar soil typically refers to only the finer fraction of lunar regolith, which is composed of grains 1 cm in diameter or less, but is often used interchangeably. Lunar soil differs substantially in properties from terrestrial soil. Lunar dust is even finer regolith than lunar soil, with grain sizes less than one millimeter."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg/330px-Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_regolith","wikidata":"Q1037499"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg/500px-Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg/1280px-Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg/500px-Buzz_Aldrin%27s_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Buzz Aldrin's bootprint on lunar soil was formed during the 1969 Apollo 11 mission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuzz_Aldrin's_bootprint_on_the_Moon%2C_AS11-40-5877.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Lunar_Regolith_70050_from_Apollo_17_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg/500px-Lunar_Regolith_70050_from_Apollo_17_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Lunar_Regolith_70050_from_Apollo_17_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg/1280px-Lunar_Regolith_70050_from_Apollo_17_in_National_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip